   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the real property law, in relation to enacting the electric\nvehicle rights act",
    "basePrintNo": "S8518",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8518-2021",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Art 9-D §343, RP L",
    "lawSection": "Real Property Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8518\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to enacting the elec-\ntric vehicle rights act\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo remove barriers to the installation of electric vehicle charging\nstations.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the real property law by adding article 9-C, which\nprohibits homeowners' associations from adopting or enforcing any rules\nor regulations that would effectively prohibit the installation or use\nof electric vehicle charging stations, or render the installation overly\ncostly.  Any denial of a homeowner's application to install an electric\nvehicle charger must be in writing and contain a detailed description of\nthe reasons for denial. If a written denial is not issued to the home-\nowner within 60 days the application shall be deemed approved.' Attor-\nney's fees shall be awarded to a homeowner if they prevail in an action\nseeking to enforce compliance with this section.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIncreasing the adoption of electric vehicles is essential for New York\nto able to meet emissions goals set by The Climate Leadership and Commu-\nnity Protection Act (CLCPA) but without a robust charging station\nnetwork, consumers won't see electric vehicles as realistic alternatives\nto gas powered vehicles.\n\nThis legislation would prevent a homeowners' association from prohibit-\ning a homeowner from installing a charging station on their property,\nwhile also providing a homeowners' association the ability to have a\nvoice in the installation process if they choose.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n60 days after becoming law.\n",
