    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8518A\n\nSPONSOR: HINCHEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the real property law, in relation to enacting the elec-\ntric vehicle rights act\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo remove barriers to the installation of electric vehicle charging\nstations.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends the real property law by adding article 9-C, which\nprohibits homeowners' associations from adopting or enforcing any rules\nor regulations that would effectively prohibit the installation or use\nof electric vehicle charging stations, or render the installation overly\ncostly.\n\nAny denial of a homeowner's application to install an electric vehicle\ncharger must be in writing and contain a detailed description of the\nreasons for denial. If a written denial is not issued to the homeowner\nwithin 60 days the application shall be deemed approved.  Attorney's\nfees shall be awarded to a homeowner if she prevails in an action seek-\ning to enforce compliance with this section.\n\nSection 2 provides the effective date\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIncreasing the adoption of electric vehicles is essential for New York\nto able to meet emissions goals set by The Climate Leadership and Commu-\nnity Protection Act (CLCPA) but without a robust charging station\nnetwork, consumers won't see electric vehicles as realistic alternatives\nto gas powered vehicles. This legislation would prevent a homeowners'\nassociation from prohibiting a homeowner from installing a charging\nstation on her property, while also providing a homeowners' association\nthe ability to have a voice in the installation process if they choose.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n60 days after becoming law.\n",
